 able  to get more people out now arriving here  as well over the last uh day or so last  24 hours they've also been people who've  as a result of the ceasefire being able  to get out of the city of mariopol  and they are bringing with them some  really terrible stories of atrocities  and terror inside that city  much of it has been  destroyed uh it's really destroyed  obliterated beyond recognition  in many places i was speaking to one  woman who was telling me that in the  neighborhood she lived in  you'd have apartment blocks and then  between those apartment blocks there  would be cemeteries where uh people  would have to bury their neighbors  because they'd been killed in the uh in  the fighting so an extremely difficult  time uh for the people of mariopol but  there are still lots of people in the  city not just in as of style the city's  population before the war was about 450  000  possibly a bit more  we think there's about 100 000 people  left in the city  but extraordinarily difficult  for those people that remain  and alex what happens to them now  they've been through so much to get  there we can see them talking to various  people around them obviously there's  media there that want to hear their  stories but where do they go  next  well i think in  in many cases they'll go and stay with  friends and families  family others will head abroad and  become as five million people have in  this country they will become uh  refugees you can see these people  getting off now they've got some of  their things with them  but they'll be heading over here we'll  just move the camera around now and i'll  show you you can see in the background  uh you can see a tent over there that  will be a registration uh point where  their details will be taken by  uh the un they'll also be given if they  need it some medical treatment there  as well and they can be given  homes within ukraine as well but i think  lots of people it's quite an unnerving  experience for lots of these people  because they will be arriving here but  they won't really in some cases be  knowing where exactly they are going at  this point  so not only have they they had to deal  with the trauma of leaving mario paul  and the steel works in some cases i  should point out that some of the people  on this coach have been picked up on the  way in the city as a result of the  ceasefire not everybody that we're  seeing here has been trapped inside the  as of style steel works  but they also now of course have the  unknown in some cases of where exactly  they will be staying but you can see  that there is a huge amount of interest  in this story uh press from all over the  world i mean the world's attention  really has been for the last few months  on the city of mariopol and also of  course on that steel work where people  are trapped in inside and we've seen  we've seen some terrible  pictures from inside that place that  have been posted on social media  civilians fighters  uh in dire need of emergency medical  treatment people who've lost limbs uh  people with wounds that are gangrenous  and have gone septic  and of course they're not getting the  attention and the aid they need they  need but here  we are seeing some people who've  have managed to get out so  in terms of ukraine  this is being seen  in many places really as a symbol  of hope in what has been a really dark  uh period  for the country  but lots of people getting off some of  them fairly unsteady on their feet  uncertain of course i mean it must be  quite disorientating  the time they've had remember of course  that the journey for these people if  they have left the as of store steel  works would have started on saturday we  saw pictures of people coming out of the  rubble  extremely dangerous remember they were  only able to get out because there was  this uh temporary ceasefire and we've  seen and they will have known know this  too we've seen these ceasefires break  down all the time so you'll be 